    {
      "id": "p5",
      "number": 5,
      "title": "Problem 5",
      "sourcePages": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A skier wants to ski down from the top of a mountain to its base. There are several possible routes, using different slopes en route, and passing through some flat areas. For each slope, there is a maximum advisable speed. The effort required to ski down a slope depends upon the length of the slope and the speed of skiing and is given by the following formula: $e$ = $d$ × (60 − $s$) if $s$ ≤ 50, and $e$ = $d$ × ($s$ − 40) if $s >$ 50, where $e$ is the effort required, $d$ is the distance travelled, and $s$ is the speed of travel. Travelling across a flat area requires no effort."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In each of the problems below, you are given a map of the mountain slopes—that is, the list of flat areas and details about the slopes connecting these areas. Note that one can only ski downwards on a slope. For each slope, you are given the flat areas that it connects, the length of the slope and the maximum advisable speed for it."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "You have to determine the minimum total effort that the skier has to expend in order to reach the mountain base, while staying within the maximum advisable speed at every slope."
        }
      ],
